{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 10,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.941,
            0.05,
            0.988,
            0.07
          ],
          "content": "115"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.083,
            0.744,
            0.148
          ],
          "content": "**Compare** the speeds of the trains. Which is the fastest train? The train which has covered the maximum distance in unit time is the fastest train, that is, the one with the highest speed."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.161,
            0.743,
            0.207
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "8.3.1 Relationship between speed, distance, and time"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.214,
            0.577,
            0.234
          ],
          "content": "We already know how to calculate speed using"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.239,
            0.421,
            0.283
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm { S p e e d } = { \\frac { \\mathrm { T o t a l ~ d i s t a n c e ~ c o v e r e d } } { \\mathrm { T o t a l ~ t i m e ~ t a k e n } } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.288,
            0.744,
            0.368
          ],
          "content": "if the distance travelled and time taken for it are known to us. We can write this equation in a different form to calculate the distance covered by an object, if we know its speed and the time taken, by using"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.372,
            0.687,
            0.392
          ],
          "content": "\\mathbf { T o t a l \\_ d i s t a n c e \\_ c o v e r e d - S p e e d } \\times \\mathbf { T o t a l \\_ t i m e \\_ t a k e n }",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.396,
            0.744,
            0.435
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, we can also calculate the time an object will take to cover a distance, if the distance and speed are given, by using"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.212,
            0.44,
            0.643,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": "Total time taken  = \\frac{ Total distance covered }{ speed }",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.482,
            0.744,
            0.541
          ],
          "content": "**Example 8.2:** Raghav is going to a neighbouring city in a bus moving at a speed of 50 km/h. If it takes him 2 h to reach that city, how far is that city?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.543,
            0.607,
            0.564
          ],
          "content": "**Solution:** Distance covered by bus = Speed × Time"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.44,
            0.569,
            0.564,
            0.621
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{50\\  km }{\\pi} \\times 2\\  K  = 100\\  km",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.625,
            0.743,
            0.665
          ],
          "content": "**Example 8.3:** A train is travelling at a speed of 90 km/h. How much time will it take to cover a distance of 360 km?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.67,
            0.637,
            0.708
          ],
          "content": "**Solution:** Time taken by the"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.394,
            0.669,
            0.636,
            0.786
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{train} &= \\frac{\\mathrm{Distance{它}{overed}}}{\\mathrm{Specd}} \\\\&= \\frac{360\\mathrm{km}}{90\\frac{\\mathrm{km}}{\\mathrm{h}}} \\\\&= 4\\mathrm{h}\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.794,
            0.745,
            0.928
          ],
          "content": "In all the examples so far, we have found the speed of an object by using ‘the total distance covered divided by the total time taken’. However, the object might not have travelled with the same speed during the entire duration of time. The object might have sometimes moved slower or sometimes faster. So, the speed that we have calculated is the **average speed**, but, in this book, we have used the term ‘speed’ for ‘average speed’."
        },
        {
          "type": "aside_text",
          "bbox": [
            0.958,
            0.119,
            0.98,
            0.345
          ],
          "content": "Measurement of Time and Motion"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
